      "title": "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given the Center and a Point on the Circle"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186087768/plots/1b185db4ea48fadce47df3ffeca3e8f2681c17ee342467c31242e3cf623973c6.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and the point $X(-3,2)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(-3-(- \\frac{1}{2}))}^{2}+{(2-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(- \\frac{5}{2})^{2}+{\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(- \\frac{1}{2}))^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2} & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2})^{2} \\\\ (x+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/id": "5d88ce8b-001f-5d81-9765-6fc788e11dae",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "990b75e5-a4e2-5b68-aa3c-70b48d5ed563",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
