    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186087768/plots/1d4af73f61e5b2180c13d8f2fee2013b3cf5f3fd790d334e2679f3923a9de7f7.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(\\frac{9}{2},- \\frac{9}{2})$ and the point $X(0,-1)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(0-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+{(-1-(- \\frac{9}{2}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(- \\frac{9}{2})^{2}+{\\frac{7}{2}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{65}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{130}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y-(- \\frac{9}{2}))^{2} & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{130}}{2})^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{9}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{65}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/id": "c02295fa-261c-5ad0-b4fa-11e66de8b020",
